/-!
# The ordinary symmetric-five factor splits over its alternating core

The actual S₅ marking of V₂/C_V₂(G₂) identifies F*(G₂), followed by its
original inclusion in G, with A₅. The quotient projection is equivariant
for this exact identification; its kernel is fixed by A₅, and A₅ acts
trivially on V/V₂ by the exceptional first-factor geometry.

Vanishing H¹ of the natural A₅ module lifts through the central kernel
because A₅ is perfect. This gives V = V₂ + C_V(A₅). Dual H¹ vanishing
separates [V₂,A₅] from that kernel. The natural quotient is generated by
commutators and has no fixed vectors, so V = [V,A₅] ⊕ C_V(A₅). This is
precisely SplitAtSecond, and contradicts the nonsplit hypothesis.

All cohomology and model facts are proved inputs. No universal earlier-source
implication, fixed-supplement premise, or transfer of offender generation
from V₂ to V is used. In fact neither the FF hypothesis nor the equality
of the supplied factor space with its moved space is needed for this row.
The final exclusion supplies the hfive premise of
ordinary_secondCase_of_exclusion_and_fixed_kernel; that consumer's assembly
owns the import of this leaf.

Source: Aschbacher–Smith, combined manuscript (1 March 2004), the concluding
proof of B.5.6, printed p.110, using B.5.1(2), B.5.11 and I.1.6(1).
-/

public section

open Subgroup Representation Representation.BinaryOffender Representation.OneCohomology
open CFSG.Quasithin.ChapterB.QuasisimpleSource
